From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm latent: TF_GLM_OVN_LATENT_TILE register-tiled absorb/expand (byte identical), G=2 arm

The prompt absorb_q / expand_v reload q / o grid.y times (16x at the serve
shape, the dominant DRAM term) because each 32-column strip is its own block.
The tile kernels put G strips in one block and stage q / o in shared once a
row, so the big input tensor is read G times fewer. Each 128-thread strip is
exactly one base block, so every output keeps its fma chain, lane butterfly
and cross-warp tree: bit identical to tf_glm_absorb_q / tf_glm_expand_v.

Ported from ai-engine fork-ssh/ovn-up's ovn-latile at b2d1754ab (the same
change as e5fd9cdc5 on ovn-numx). Two edits: the kernel header now
instantiates G=2 as well as G=4 (tf_glm_absorb_tile_g2 / tf_glm_expand_tile_g2
and their reversed-butterfly controls), because G=2 with two blocks an SM is
the one LATENT_TILE variant the fork never built; and the routing is expressed
over this series' absorbFn / expandFn (LATENT_ROWS x2/x4), which the fork's
branch did not have.

TF_GLM_OVN_LATENT_TILE: unset or 0 off, 4 the fork's form (512 threads, one
block a SM), 2 the new form (256 threads, two blocks an SM); prompt rows only,
16-byte aligned q / o / kv_b; LATENT_ROWS, ABSORB_VEC and KVB8 keep their
paths. Receipts: host tests of the knob row, of routing and precedence and of
the launch shapes (kernel id and block x at both G), and glm-latent-tile-check
/ -bench (GPU): both G against today's byte for byte at the serve shapes with
reversed-butterfly controls that must differ, then base / vec / G=4 / G=2
timed.

